WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:03.910
Imagine a leader who gives a genuinely inspiring talk to

2
00:00:03.910 --> 00:00:06.670
the whole division. The goal is bold: every notice people

3
00:00:06.670 --> 00:00:09.630
receive will be written in plain language by spring. The

4
00:00:09.630 --> 00:00:12.650
room is energized. Three months later, very little has changed,

5
00:00:12.690 --> 00:00:14.050
and staff are quietly skeptical.

6
00:00:15.020 --> 00:00:16.740
I think many of us have been in that room,

7
00:00:16.740 --> 00:00:19.540
and then lived through the three months that followed. So

8
00:00:19.540 --> 00:00:22.540
what does the page mean by charismatic leadership, and why

9
00:00:22.540 --> 00:00:23.940
does that gap matter so much?

10
00:00:25.530 --> 00:00:29.090
Charismatic leadership draws its force from the leader's personal conviction,

11
00:00:29.090 --> 00:00:33.010
presence and story. People follow not only because of a

12
00:00:33.010 --> 00:00:35.710
title, but because the leader makes the work feel urgent

13
00:00:35.710 --> 00:00:40.410
and meaningful. These leaders are meaning makers. They can take

14
00:00:40.410 --> 00:00:43.090
a complicated situation and turn it into a clear story

15
00:00:43.090 --> 00:00:44.570
about why the work matters.

16
00:00:45.760 --> 00:00:48.680
That sounds like a real gift, honestly. Public service can

17
00:00:48.680 --> 00:00:51.040
feel routine, and someone who reminds people why they chose

18
00:00:51.040 --> 00:00:52.840
this work could make a big difference.

19
00:00:53.570 --> 00:00:57.030
It can. The page also names the trade-off. Because the

20
00:00:57.030 --> 00:01:00.290
influence is tied to one person, trust and loyalty flow

21
00:01:00.290 --> 00:01:03.370
to that individual rather than to shared processes or the

22
00:01:03.370 --> 00:01:05.870
wider team. That creates a lot of energy in the

23
00:01:05.870 --> 00:01:08.590
short term, and real fragility over time. If the leader

24
00:01:08.690 --> 00:01:10.610
moves on, the momentum often leaves with them.

25
00:01:11.300 --> 00:01:13.940
What would I notice about a charismatic leader in an

26
00:01:13.940 --> 00:01:17.240
ordinary month? I imagine it goes beyond big speeches at

27
00:01:17.240 --> 00:01:17.880
all staff meetings.

28
00:01:18.710 --> 00:01:23.010
Vivid stories and memorable language instead of dry memos. A

29
00:01:23.010 --> 00:01:25.430
strong presence at town halls and in front of outside

30
00:01:25.430 --> 00:01:30.070
partners. Bold goals that challenge long accepted habits. A real

31
00:01:30.070 --> 00:01:33.590
ability to read the room, so people feel seen. And

32
00:01:33.590 --> 00:01:36.990
sometimes loyalty framed in personal terms, along the lines of,

33
00:01:36.990 --> 00:01:38.290
are you with me on this?

34
00:01:38.920 --> 00:01:42.320
Where does this style do its best work? It seems

35
00:01:42.320 --> 00:01:45.520
especially valuable when a team has grown tired or cynical.

36
00:01:46.250 --> 00:01:49.510
It is very good at breaking inertia. In a large

37
00:01:49.510 --> 00:01:51.770
agency where people have heard that is how we have

38
00:01:51.770 --> 00:01:55.010
always done it many times, a careful memo rarely changes

39
00:01:55.010 --> 00:01:58.410
anything. A charismatic leader can reignite pride in the mission

40
00:01:58.410 --> 00:02:02.270
and persuade skeptical partners, legislators or community groups to back

41
00:02:02.270 --> 00:02:02.850
a new direction.

42
00:02:03.740 --> 00:02:06.480
And the opening story shows us the risk. Where does

43
00:02:06.480 --> 00:02:08.840
the page say this style tends to break down, even

44
00:02:08.840 --> 00:02:09.480
with good intentions?

45
00:02:10.350 --> 00:02:14.790
In the gap between inspiration and execution. Exciting a room

46
00:02:14.790 --> 00:02:18.410
is far easier than building the budgets, systems, staffing and

47
00:02:18.410 --> 00:02:22.210
accountability that deliver the promise. Charismatic leaders can also attract

48
00:02:22.210 --> 00:02:25.590
people who agree with them, while the detail oriented colleagues

49
00:02:25.590 --> 00:02:27.110
who point out risks are sidelined.

50
00:02:27.510 --> 00:02:31.570
Over repeated cycles of big words and thin delivery, people

51
00:02:31.570 --> 00:02:33.510
become more cynical than they were before.

52
00:02:34.360 --> 00:02:37.960
Let's talk about fair service and staff. The page describes

53
00:02:37.960 --> 00:02:41.460
charismatic leadership's effect on equity as high visibility and high

54
00:02:41.460 --> 00:02:43.380
volatility. What does that mean in practice?

55
00:02:44.250 --> 00:02:46.870
On the positive side, a leader who speaks with real

56
00:02:46.870 --> 00:02:50.410
conviction about disability rights, racial equity and human dignity can

57
00:02:50.410 --> 00:02:53.070
move equity from a side project to the center of

58
00:02:53.070 --> 00:02:56.230
the organization. They can open doors with community partners and

59
00:02:56.230 --> 00:02:58.270
attract talented people who want to work for a visible

60
00:02:58.270 --> 00:02:59.190
champion of inclusion.

61
00:03:00.060 --> 00:03:03.220
And the risk, I imagine, is what happens to hope

62
00:03:03.220 --> 00:03:06.720
when the follow-through does not arrive. That seems especially painful

63
00:03:06.720 --> 00:03:08.420
for the people who were counting on it most.

64
00:03:09.230 --> 00:03:12.530
Exactly. When a stirring commitment is not backed by the

65
00:03:12.530 --> 00:03:16.870
unglamorous work of revising hiring criteria, funding captions and assistive

66
00:03:16.870 --> 00:03:20.670
technology, and holding managers accountable, the people who hoped most

67
00:03:20.670 --> 00:03:23.090
feel betrayed. There is also a quieter risk.

68
00:03:23.510 --> 00:03:27.490
Charismatic cultures can start to equate leadership with being outgoing

69
00:03:27.490 --> 00:03:32.530
and polished on stage, which overlooks quieter leaders, neurodivergent colleagues,

70
00:03:33.230 --> 00:03:35.430
people still learning English, and Deaf staff.

71
00:03:36.480 --> 00:03:39.020
So what should the leader from our opening do now,

72
00:03:39.020 --> 00:03:43.040
three months later, with a skeptical room? Another inspiring speech

73
00:03:43.040 --> 00:03:44.640
seems like the wrong move.

74
00:03:45.990 --> 00:03:48.830
Acknowledge the gap honestly, name what got in the way,

75
00:03:49.130 --> 00:03:51.990
and commit to one small, visible first step, with a

76
00:03:51.990 --> 00:03:55.870
date and someone responsible. Honesty rebuilds trust, and a concrete

77
00:03:55.870 --> 00:03:58.970
step turns the vision into work people can see. Another

78
00:03:58.970 --> 00:04:01.570
rousing talk without follow through would deepen the skepticism.

79
00:04:02.620 --> 00:04:05.320
That feels like advice that would help anyone with a

80
00:04:05.320 --> 00:04:07.800
big vision, not only people who think of themselves as

81
00:04:07.800 --> 00:04:10.280
charismatic. What else does the page suggest?

82
00:04:11.070 --> 00:04:14.290
Tie every public commitment to a named owner, a funded

83
00:04:14.290 --> 00:04:17.070
plan and a way to measure it. Make sure leadership

84
00:04:17.070 --> 00:04:20.570
is recognized for quiet competence and fair results, not only

85
00:04:20.570 --> 00:04:23.810
stage presence. And share the microphone early, with front line

86
00:04:23.810 --> 00:04:27.470
staff and operational leaders, so the mission belongs to the

87
00:04:27.470 --> 00:04:29.850
organization rather than to one personality.

88
00:04:30.480 --> 00:04:33.160
I like that framing a lot. The energy is a

89
00:04:33.160 --> 00:04:35.540
real gift, and the work is making sure that energy

90
00:04:35.540 --> 00:04:37.280
outlasts the person who brought it.

91
00:04:38.350 --> 00:04:41.370
Well said. Here is a question to carry with you.

92
00:04:41.990 --> 00:04:44.550
Where has your message run ahead of the support behind

93
00:04:44.550 --> 00:04:47.030
it, and what is one step that would close that

94
00:04:47.030 --> 00:04:48.890
gap? Thank you for listening.
